course: dictionary
lesson: 4026
title: Gator, two meanings in context
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A gator is a short, informal name for an alligator, a large reptile with strong jaws that lives in rivers and swamps. People use it a lot in the USA, especially in Florida and Louisiana. Context helps you work out which meaning is right.
explain: Read the clues around the word. «In the dry season alligators dig gator holes that keep water when everything else dries out, and many other animals drink and live there.» The sentence tells you what a gator hole does, and the alligators are the ones digging. The clues point to the reptile meaning.
explain: Gator can also mean a small off-road farm or utility vehicle, from a brand name. Ask: what word would fit here? A reptile in a swamp, or a little vehicle on a farm? The clues in the sentence decide.
explain: Always stay safe. The phrase «watch out for gators» is a warning: never feed or go near gators, and stay away from the water's edge in gator areas.
fact: A gator is short for alligator, a large reptile with strong jaws.
fact: Gator is informal, so you use it in chatty speech, not in formal writing.
fact: «Gator holes» are holes that keep water when everything else dries out in the dry season, and other animals drink there too.
fact: A gator can also mean a small off-road farm or utility vehicle, from a brand name.
fact: Never feed or go near gators, and stay away from the water's edge in gator areas.
exercise: «In the dry season alligators dig gator holes that keep water when everything else dries out.» What does gator mean here? | A type of crocodile sold in shops | *A large reptile, short for alligator | A small farm vehicle | A hole full of mud
exercise: Dad says «watch out for gators» at the lake. What should you do? | *Stay away from the water's edge | Pet them gently | Jump in and swim across | Feed them some bread
exercise: Sam sees a little vehicle on the farm and shouts, «It's a gator!» What does gator mean here? | *A small off-road farm or utility vehicle | A large reptile with strong jaws | A type of crocodile | A kind of swamp
test: Which word is a short, informal name for alligator? | Gaiter | Gradient | Gargle | *Gator
test: «See you later, alligator — in a while, crocodile» is a rhyming phrase. What does it mean? | A question about the time | *A cheerful goodbye, like see you soon | A warning about reptiles | A rule for swimming
exam: Why should you never feed or go near gators? | They only eat plants | They are shy and like company | They are dogs that bite | *They are wild animals that can be dangerous, so keep your distance
